# Section 2: Types and Branches of AI

## 2.1 Machine Learning

📘 **Machine Learning (ML)** is a branch of AI in which algorithms learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules (Mitchell, 1997).

Three main styles:

| Style | How it learns | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Supervised learning** | From labeled examples (input + correct answer) | Email → spam/not spam |
| **Unsupervised learning** | Finds structure in unlabeled data | Grouping customers by buying habits |
| **Reinforcement learning** | Trial and error with rewards and penalties | A program learning to play a game |

## 2.2 Deep Learning

📘 **Deep Learning** is a subset of machine learning using **neural networks** with many layers. 📘 A **neural network** is a model made of connected units ("neurons") that pass numbers forward, loosely inspired by the brain (Goodfellow et al., 2016). Deep learning excels at images, audio, and language.

## 2.3 Generative AI

📘 **Generative AI** creates new content (text, images, audio, code) that resembles its training data. Examples include chatbots and image generators. Many modern systems are built on the **transformer** architecture (Vaswani et al., 2017).

## 2.4 Relationship Between AI Technologies

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    AI["Artificial Intelligence (broadest)"]
    ML["Machine Learning"]
    DL["Deep Learning"]
    GEN["Generative AI (e.g., large language models)"]
    AI --> ML --> DL --> GEN
```

Think of nested circles: **all** generative AI used today is deep learning, all deep learning is machine learning, and all machine learning is AI. The reverse is not true: a hand-written chess program is AI but not machine learning.

