# Section 3: The History and Evolution of AI

## 3.1 The Beginning of Artificial Intelligence

In 1950, Alan Turing asked "Can machines think?" and proposed what became known as the **Turing Test** (Turing, 1950). The term **"artificial intelligence"** was introduced in the 1955 proposal for the 1956 Dartmouth workshop (McCarthy et al., 1955/2006).

## 3.2 Major Stages of AI Development

| Period | Milestone |
|---|---|
| 1950 | Turing's paper on machine intelligence |
| 1956 | Dartmouth workshop; AI named as a field |
| 1958 | Rosenblatt's perceptron, an early neural network |
| 1966 | ELIZA, an early conversational program (Weizenbaum, 1966) |
| 1970s | First "AI winter" after unmet expectations |
| 1980s | Expert systems become commercial; second AI winter follows |
| 1986 | Backpropagation popularized (Rumelhart et al., 1986) |
| 1997 | IBM Deep Blue defeats world chess champion Garry Kasparov |
| 2012 | AlexNet shows deep learning's power in image recognition (Krizhevsky et al., 2012) |
| 2016 | AlphaGo defeats a top Go player (Silver et al., 2016) |
| 2017 | Transformer architecture introduced (Vaswani et al., 2017) |
| 2020s | Large language models and generative AI reach the general public |

## 3.3 Challenges Throughout AI History

- 📘 **AI winter:** a period of reduced funding and interest after hype outpaced results (Lighthill, 1973; Minsky & Papert, 1969, on limits of early perceptrons).
- **Limited computing power and data** in early decades.
- **Brittleness:** early rule-based systems failed outside narrow situations.
- **Ethical and social concerns** that continue today.

