# Section 6: The Evolution of Chatbots

📘 A **chatbot** is a program that converses with people through text or voice.

| Generation | Method | Strengths | Weaknesses | Example |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **1. Rule-based** | Pattern matching and scripted replies | Predictable, simple | Breaks with unexpected input | ELIZA (1966) |
| **2. Machine learning** | Classifies a user's *intent* from examples | Handles varied wording | Needs labeled data; limited scope | Customer-service bots |
| **3. Deep learning** | Neural networks model sentences | Better language understanding | Needs lots of data and computing power | Early voice assistants |
| **4. Large language model (LLM)** | Transformer trained on huge text collections to predict the next word | Flexible, fluent, many tasks | Can produce confident errors ("hallucinations") | Modern AI assistants |

### 6.1 First Generation: Rule-Based Chatbots
ELIZA matched keywords and rephrased them as questions. Many users felt it understood them even though it did not (Weizenbaum, 1966). This is called the **ELIZA effect**.

### 6.2 Second Generation: Machine Learning Chatbots
These classify what the user wants (e.g., "check order status") from many example phrases, then return a prepared response.

### 6.3 Third Generation: Deep Learning Chatbots
Neural networks, especially sequence models, improved translation, speech recognition, and reply quality.

### 6.4 Fourth Generation: Large Language Model Chatbots
📘 A **Large Language Model (LLM)** is a neural network trained on very large amounts of text to predict likely next words, which lets it write, summarize, translate, and answer questions (Brown et al., 2020). Because LLMs generate plausible text rather than looking up verified facts, users should check important answers.

**✏️ Quick activity:** Ask a rule-based-style FAQ bot and an LLM assistant the same unusual question. Record how each responds. *Expected outcome:* the rule-based bot often fails or gives a canned reply; the LLM gives a flexible answer that may still contain errors.

