# Section 9: AI Development and Evaluation in Industry

## 9.1 AI Applications in Industrial Automation
Examples: robots guided by vision, predictive maintenance (forecasting when a machine will fail), and supply-chain forecasting.

## 9.2 AI in Manufacturing and Quality Control
Cameras and image-recognition models inspect products for defects faster and more consistently than manual checks in many cases, and they can flag unusual items for human review.

## 9.3 Evaluating AI Performance in Industrial Systems
The measures from Section 5.4 apply directly. In quality control, a **false negative** (defect passed as good) may reach customers; a **false positive** (good item rejected) wastes money. Industries also track **reliability**, **response time**, **safety**, and **cost**.

## 9.4 Case Study: Tesla Self-Driving Technology
📘 The **SAE levels of driving automation** range from Level 0 (no automation) to Level 5 (full automation) (SAE International, 2021). Tesla's Autopilot and "Full Self-Driving (Supervised)" features use cameras and neural networks, but they are generally classified as **driver-assistance** features that require the driver to stay attentive and ready to take over. Safety regulators in the United States have examined crashes involving such systems (NHTSA, n.d.).

