# Glossary

| Term | Definition |
|---|---|
| **Accuracy** | The share of all predictions that are correct. |
| **AI alignment** | Making AI systems pursue goals consistent with human values and intentions. |
| **AI winter** | A period of reduced funding and enthusiasm in AI after unmet expectations. |
| **Algorithm** | A step-by-step procedure for solving a problem. |
| **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** | The field of building systems that perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence. |
| **Backpropagation** | A method for computing how much each weight in a neural network contributed to the error. |
| **Bias (in AI)** | Systematic unfairness or skew in an AI system's results. |
| **Chatbot** | A program that converses with people via text or voice. |
| **Confusion matrix** | A table counting true/false positives and negatives. |
| **Dataset** | A collection of examples used to train or test a model. |
| **Deep learning** | Machine learning using many-layered neural networks. |
| **Embedding** | A list of numbers representing the meaning of text, images, or other items. |
| **Epoch** | One full pass through the training data. |
| **F1 score** | A single score balancing precision and recall. |
| **Feature** | A measurable piece of input data. |
| **Generative AI** | AI that creates new content such as text, images, or audio. |
| **Gradient descent** | Adjusting parameters step by step to reduce loss. |
| **Hallucination** | A fluent but false or invented output from a generative model. |
| **Human-in-the-loop** | A design where a person reviews or approves AI decisions. |
| **Label** | The correct answer attached to a training example. |
| **Large Language Model (LLM)** | A neural network trained on large text collections to predict and generate language. |
| **Learning rate** | The size of each adjustment step during training. |
| **Loss** | A number measuring how wrong a model's predictions are. |
| **Machine learning** | Algorithms that learn patterns from data. |
| **Model** | A mathematical representation that maps inputs to predictions. |
| **Narrow AI** | AI designed for specific tasks. |
| **Neural network** | A model of connected units that pass numbers through layers. |
| **Overfitting** | When a model memorizes training data and fails on new data. |
| **Parameter (weight)** | An adjustable number inside a model. |
| **Precision** | Of the items flagged positive, the share truly positive. |
| **Prompt injection** | An attack that hides instructions in input to manipulate an AI system. |
| **Recall** | Of all truly positive items, the share the model found. |
| **Recommendation system** | A system that predicts items a user may like. |
| **Reinforcement learning** | Learning by trial and error using rewards and penalties. |
| **Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)** | Retrieving relevant documents first, then generating an answer from them. |
| **Rule-based system** | A system that follows human-written if–then rules. |
| **SAE levels** | A scale from 0 to 5 describing driving automation. |
| **Supervised learning** | Learning from labeled examples. |
| **Test set** | Data kept separate to measure final performance. |
| **Training** | Adjusting a model's parameters using data. |
| **Transformer** | A neural network design that handles sequences, such as text, using attention. |
| **Underfitting** | When a model is too simple to capture the pattern. |
| **Unsupervised learning** | Finding structure in unlabeled data. |
| **Validation set** | Data used to tune a model during development. |

